#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092089 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "974a88f6-ff6a-5183-aeaf-6019e09061ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Petal of a Polar Rose"}] :question/id #uuid "0d1dcc59-e7af-5a86-87ba-aa7ee1b23767" :question/math-academy-id "e-632" :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r = \\cos(3\\theta)$ and the lines $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](images/35/350a721c254dab3a8acb2ae54ca5be0bcde6ab036e6936939194c57ae55fe4ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = \\cos(3\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092090 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c9f0f1-be39-5893-b757-7a4750b07ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by the Loop of a Limaçon"}] :question/id #uuid "6040d180-ed1d-52b8-809c-b5820352d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-633" :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r = 1+2\\cos\\theta$ for $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6bfa0990b560bcb09f2a9eb0b9542296f2a8f1c196b6740702e81c59941d66c5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 1+2\\cos(\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+2\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -4\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -3\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092105 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092268 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Areas Bounded by Other Polar Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d8dca5-a0f3-5b6f-81f5-4eea46ee70d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6822" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7b39805131dc562e233d49a84f69d30e80ca29cb9443012032d18fdb4512054.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta,$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c9f0f1-be39-5893-b757-7a4750b07ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by the Loop of a Limaçon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8608a1b5-916c-5a28-968d-f8e05bc0df59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π - 8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090052} {:answer/id #uuid "a481b653-7650-59b1-96ad-f6086267763e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π + 4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090053} {:answer/id #uuid "396bebd6-29a3-5118-9b15-759108e8bd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π + 8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090054} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5192de-9e52-5bda-a4db-ca206505d649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090055} {:answer/id #uuid "743c7156-015a-56cb-aa3b-a8e3c18b85ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π + 8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090056}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3d48976e-ecd9-52fe-8371-edaabd05c065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090051}] :question/id #uuid "cb2c5d25-e3ba-5c5d-9450-482e68e4a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-20640" :question/problem "![](images/50/50fdc7810335633b8f7f4a6f4f7a73f24941367c07854b3f276f475a0307e3d9.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r = 2 + 2\\cos θ$ for $\\frac{π}{2} \\le θ \\le π$, as shown above.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092268 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Areas Bounded by Other Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1722fb6a-3828-5c7b-bfd2-cfed88f52b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1722fb6a-3828-5c7b-bfd2-cfed88f52b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319458} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b14a60-2547-5577-9014-84c75fd158de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afbc3611-2f90-5c6e-b140-8fc556720700" :question/math-academy-id "q-352330" :question/problem "![](images/32/327f1f7a395616de463c054ec904812dcd422776522a262f956530d3aa9278eb.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\theta }^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\pi }^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
